      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/01-rays/potamotrygon-wallacei-2/",
      "html": "<p>P. wallacei is or was the most often imported species of Potamotrygon from Brazil. Until recently the fish were wrongly identified as P. histrix, a different species that is not kept in aquaria at all and occurs in southern South America. The type specimen of P. histrix was collected in Argentina.</p><p>Since January 2017 all species of Potamotrygon from Brazil and Colombia have to be exported only with CITES certificates, as these countries have placed the fish on appendix III of CITES. The species can still be traded, but breeders now have to demonstrate that their bred specimens are not descended from illegally imported fish from Brazil or Colombia when they want to export their animals out of the EU. Most bred specimens, however, do not stay in the EU because the market here is much too small.</p><p>It took about one year for the authorities to react to the new situation and to supply the ornamental fish exporters with the CITES certificates needed for legal exportation.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/01-rays/potamotrygon-wallacei-2/",
      "html": "<p>This freshwater stingray lives endemically in the Rio Negro system in Brazil. For aquarium purposes it is usually collected around Barcelos.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/01-rays/potamotrygon-wallacei-2/",
      "html": "<p>P. wallacei is the best suited species of Potamotrygon for private aquaria, because it is the smallest species of the genus. The disc diameter hardly ever exceeds 30 cm. This means it still needs large tanks, but most of the remaining species of freshwater stingray become at least double as large, many even much larger.</p>"
    }
  ],
